public int minOperations(int n) {
    int[] arr = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        arr[i] = (2 * i) + 1;
    }

    int minOperations = 0;
    Set<Integer> unequalIndices = new HashSet<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] != n/2) {
            unequalIndices.add(i);
        }
    }

    while (!unequalIndices.isEmpty()) {
        int closestPairIndex = -1;
        int closestPairValue = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (int i : unequalIndices) {
            for (int j : unequalIndices) {
                int difference = Math.abs(i - j);
                if (difference < closestPairValue) {
                    closestPairIndex = i;
                    closestPairValue = difference;
                }
            }
        }

        int x = closestPairIndex;
        int y = (x + closestPairValue) % n;
        if (arr[x] < arr[y]) {
            int temp = arr[x];
            arr[x] = arr[y];
            arr[y] = temp;
        }

        unequalIndices.remove(x);
        unequalIndices.remove(y);
        minOperations++;
    }

    return minOperations;
}
```
In this code, we first initialize the array `arr` with the values `(2 * i) + 1` for 0 <= i < n. We then initialize the set `unequalIndices` with the indices of the elements that are not equal to `n/2`.

In the while loop, we first find the closest pair of indices in `unequalIndices` using a brute force search. We then subtract 1 from the value of the smaller index and add 1 to the value of the larger index. This reduces the difference between the values of the two indices.

We then remove the indices that are no longer unequal from `unequalIndices` and increment the `minOperations` counter.

The termination condition for the loop is when `unequalIndices` becomes empty. The solution is returned as `minOperations + 1`.

